<span class="cat-badge">Guide &middot; Rentals</span>
<h1>Hanoi Motorbike Rental: City Commuters Rainy-Day Planning</h1>
<p>Rain in Hanoi is not a question of whether but when, and for commuters it is not something you can reschedule around the way a tourist can. Meetings start on time whether the sky is clear or the streets are sheets of water, so a rider who commutes daily on a rented motorbike needs a plan, not luck. That plan covers three things: what you carry, how you adjust your riding, and how you schedule your day when the forecast turns wet. The rental counter at 112 Nguyen Van Cu, Long Bien, Hanoi, open daily from 09:00 to 21:00, is a good place to start, because you can ask about models and gear before the next downpour arrives.</p>
<p>This guide belongs to the <a href="/hanoi-motorbike-rental/">Hanoi motorbike rental</a> hub, works alongside the <a href="/hanoi-motorbike-rental-cost/">rental cost breakdown</a>, and pairs with the answers in the <a href="/faq/">FAQ</a>. For general advice on wet roads, the <a href="/motorbike-guides/riding-a-motorbike-in-hanoi-rainy-season/">rainy season riding guide</a> covers the basics for every kind of rider; this page focuses on the commuter's version of the problem.</p>
<nav class="toc" aria-label="Table of contents">
<ul>
<li><a href="#sec-1">Why rain matters for city commuters</a></li>
<li><a href="#sec-2">How Hanoi rain behaves through the year</a></li>
